{#
  THE REPORT. A web page that happens to be printed.

  Reached at /actions/leadgenerator/report/view?entry=<id>, which is also how Playwright
  reaches it before calling page.pdf(). So what you see in a browser at A4 width is what a
  prospect gets, and designing it is a refresh rather than a re-render.

  IT READS AND NEVER CALCULATES. Every number here came out of report.json, which came out
  of lib/surface.mjs over a partition asserted to tile the page exactly. Adding anything up
  in this file would be a second implementation of a number somebody may argue with.

  THE ANNOTATED PAGE IS ONE IMAGE SHOWN THROUGH SEVERAL WINDOWS. CSS cannot break an image
  across printed pages — it overflows and is cut, or shrinks to nothing — and a homepage
  screenshot is thousands of pixels tall (jersey.com is 16,368). So each sheet holds a box
  with the image as its background, offset by one sheet's worth, which is the only way the
  block labels stay readable.

  WHAT MUST SURVIVE ANY REDESIGN: the caveats. A number this tool could not measure is
  reported as unmeasured, and a report that quietly drops that is not this tool's report.
#}
{% set pct = { } %}
{#
  A percentage, or an em dash where there is nothing to say.

  `<0.1%` AND NOT `0.0%` for anything present but smaller than the rounding can show.
  kohde.agency paints rgb(220,220,226) across 288 pixels — about seventeen square — which
  is 0.0033% of its page and rounded to a flat nought. A colour in a list of colours on the
  page cannot be none of the page, and a reader asked why it was there. Rounding a real
  measurement down to zero is the same fault as reporting an unmeasured region as empty.
#}
{% macro pc(n) %}
    {%- if n is null -%}—
    {%- elseif n > 0 and n * 100 < 0.05 -%}&lt;0.1%
    {%- else -%}{{ (n * 100)|number_format(1) }}%
    {%- endif -%}
{% endmacro %}

{# MB TO ONE PLACE, KB BELOW A MEGABYTE.

   BINARY, AND IN STEP WITH tools/audit/lib/bytes.mjs:136, which divides by 1048576 and
   calls the result MB. Decimal would be the more defensible unit and would match Chrome's
   network panel, but the same bytes are printed by the capture CLI through that function:
   two formatters means the PDF saying 4.5 MB while the console says 4.3 MB for one page.
   If this moves, that moves with it. #}
{% macro bytes(n) %}
    {%- if n is null -%}—
    {%- elseif n >= 1048576 -%}{{ (n / 1048576)|number_format(1) }}mb
    {%- else -%}{{ (n / 1024)|number_format(0) }}kb
    {%- endif -%}
{% endmacro %}

{# ONE BAR, TWO SCORES, and they are not the same kind of number: Speed is PageSpeed
   divided by ten, Technical is this tool's own bands. Drawn identically because a reader
   compares them anyway, so the difference is stated in the labels rather than hidden in
   the styling. #}
{% macro scorebar(n) %}
    <div class="r-score">
        {# ALWAYS A DECIMAL. A score of 8 and a score of 8.4 should not print in different
           shapes: `8` reads as a rounder, cheaper number than `8.0`, and beside `6.7`
           on the same cover it looks like a different kind of measurement. #}
        <div class="r-score-n">{{ n|number_format(1) }}<span>/10</span></div>
        <div class="r-score-bar" style="--r-fill: {{ n * 10 }}%"></div>
    </div>
{% endmacro %}

{# THE SAME BAR, WITH A NAME OVER IT. The cover stacks three of these and the number alone
   cannot say which is which, but a label BESIDE the bar pushed it 141px right and 141px
   short of the one the sections print — measurably not the same object. Above it, the row
   underneath is scorebar() itself, so the two cannot drift. #}
{% macro scoreline(label, n) %}
    <div class="r-scoreline">
        <p class="r-scoreline__label">{{ label }}</p>
        {{ _self.scorebar(n) }}
    </div>
{% endmacro %}

{# The bare domain, as a person would say it out loud — the scheme and the `www.` are
   punctuation a reader has to look past. The whole address is on the cover. #}
{% macro host(url) %}
    {%- set h = url|replace({'/^https?:\\/\\//': '', '/^www\\./': ''}) -%}
    {{- h|split('/')|first -}}
{% endmacro %}

{#
  THE CHANGE BETWEEN TWO FIGURES, and the arrow that goes with it.

  `mode` says how the difference is expressed, and the three are not interchangeable:

    'share'    the values are FRACTIONS of something (0.465 against 0.117) — the difference
               is in percentage POINTS, because a ratio here is nonsense and sometimes
               impossible. Routing at 0.0% against 52.0% is not an increase of any
               percentage, it is a division by nothing.
    'points'   the values are already on a scale (8.9 out of 10) — the difference is the
               plain subtraction, in points of that scale.
    'ratio'    the values are quantities (bytes, pixels) — the difference is a percentage
               of the one compared against.

  SHARES AND SCORES BOTH READ AS POINTS, so the Change column is one kind of number down
  its whole length. They were 'pt' and '%' side by side and looked like a mistake.

  `better` says which direction is an improvement: 'up', 'down', or '' for neither. Only the
  first two take colour. A segment share has no better direction — a page giving 46% to its
  footer is not winning against one giving 11%, it made a different decision.
#}
{% macro change(a, b, mode = 'ratio', better = '') %}
    {%- if a is null or b is null -%}—
    {%- else -%}
        {%- if mode == 'share' -%}{% set delta = (a - b) * 100 %}{% set unit = 'pt' %}
        {%- elseif mode == 'points' -%}{% set delta = a - b %}{% set unit = 'pt' %}
        {%- else -%}{% set delta = b == 0 ? null : (a - b) / b * 100 %}{% set unit = '%' %}
        {%- endif -%}
        {%- if delta is null -%}—
        {%- elseif delta|abs < 0.05 -%}<span class="r-change r-change--flat">no change</span>
        {%- else -%}
            {%- set up = delta > 0 -%}
            {%- set tone = better == '' ? 'flat' : ((up and better == 'up') or (not up and better == 'down') ? 'good' : 'bad') -%}
            <span class="r-change r-change--{{ tone }}">
                {{- delta|abs|number_format(1) }}{{ unit }}
                <span class="r-change__arrow">{{ up ? '▲' : '▼' }}</span></span>
        {%- endif -%}
    {%- endif -%}
{% endmacro %}
